.TH "HEE\-GIT\-TAG" "1" "" "" "HEE Tools"
.SH NAME
hee\-git\-tag \- a GPG\-signed git tag with the key that is really yours
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
hee git tag NAME \-m MESSAGE [COMMIT] [\-\-key ID] [\-\-push] [\-\-yes]
hee git tag \-\-show\-key
hee git tag help
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IP
.EX
Creates an annotated, GPG\-signed tag (\[ga]git tag \-s\[ga]) and, with \-\-push,
pushes exactly that ref. The point of the tool is the key choice: plain
\[ga]git tag \-s\[ga] asks gpg for a key whose uid matches user.email, and in this
org user.email is the shared dev\[at]tcos.us from the dotfiles gitconfig \-\-
no secret key carries that uid, so the first prod promote ended with
\[dq]gpg: skipped Spencer Butler <dev\[at]tcos.us>: No secret key\[dq] (2026\-09\-06).

Dry run by default: prints the key, the tag, the commit and the message
and changes nothing. \-\-yes creates the tag; \-\-push pushes it.
.EE
.SH FILES
.IP
.EX
\[ti]/.hee/index/_.yaml    SOA anchor; only \[ga]host:\[ga] is read, for the tie\-break
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.IP
.EX
0 OK, 2 CRITICAL (no key, ambiguous key, git or gpg failed), 3 UNKNOWN
(gpg missing, not a git repo).
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
hee git tag \-\-show\-key
hee git tag v1.2.0 \-m \[dq]release 1.2.0\[dq] \-\-push

Not marked \[dq]# ci\[dq]: \-\-show\-key needs a signing key configured (a CI runner
has none) and tagging signs and pushes. Both are host/identity state, not
a self\-contained run.

hee git tag prod/tcos\-www/20260906T0806Z \-m \[dq]prod promotion: tcos.us\[dq] e7beec0 \-\-yes \-\-push
hee git tag \-\-show\-key
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP
